  {
    "episode_id": 47118,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "You know, I like answers like that, I guess, as somebody who despite my critics now still thinks of themselves as a, I still think of myself as a conservative, but I like it when we talk about federalism, right? Because sometimes, sometimes, you know, the law doesn't produce the outcomes we want, and then we can change the law, but there's certain, first of all, America was set up as a federalist republic, which means state's right. So your vote on same-sex marriage didn't say that North Dakota has to legalize same-sex marriage to the Supreme Court be overturned. It just said that the relationship between the states when it comes to be marriage, Congress is going to regulate that as per the Constitution. And North Dakota will recognize a marriage license issued in Iowa or Minnesota. To me, that's a federalist position. Doug Bergam was making this argument to the other day, vis-a-vis the abortion issue, you know, he was asked, would you pursue a national abortion ban? His answer was a flat. No, I won't because it's up to the states. We spent so long talking, conservatives have spent so long talking about how Roe v. Wade was a bad decision because it usurped states rights. So why would he support the federal government usurping states rights again by taking away their ability to with a federal law of which I think would be of dubious constitutionality? You know, how can you usurp states rights again? So I talk about that a little bit because there seems to be a lot of people who are very active in the Republic of Party these days, who seem to value the immediacy of results over, you know, sort of a more principled take, like, we'll know that the system is supposed to operate certain ways.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I still consider myself a conservative despite criticism.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Federalism allows states to retain autonomy in matters like marriage.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The outcome of the Supreme Court ruling on same-sex marriage did not require North Dakota to legalize same-sex marriage.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "North Dakota is required to recognize marriage licenses issued in Iowa or Minnesota under federalism.",
        "confidence": 0.91
