    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 70,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, I think, first of all, before the last election, Democrats had a five-vote majority in the House. Now Republicans have a five-vote majority in the House. The Senate was 50-50, now it's 51-49. I think if you are really, really interested in immediate gratification and willing to go bad in your principles of these things, you're going to see the pendulum shift back really, really far and really, really fast. And I know that, I mean, it goes that way, but I mean, and then the other answer is some of its the politics of it. I'm a little different, I think, on abortion and whether there could be national legislation on it. I mean, life, liberty, and happiness is the top side of it. But my problem is is I just know the political reality. You can't pass it without exceptions, which I think there should be. But I mean, this gets back to the federalism argument in a more meaningful way, Rob. I don't want to federalize rape, incest, and the exception language, because those are typically state crimes that have never seen anybody bring any kind of proposal forward that allows for state primacy on those issues with the natural ban and you have to be thoughtful about this stuff instead of just raging towards whatever gets you the most clicks are the I mean the easiest way to get people upset and angry all the time because I can promise everybody who wants a national ban are the same people who want the DOJ and the FBI to be reigned in while those two things can't necessarily be mutually exclusive if you're now federalizing rape and incest because you have to have the exceptions.",
