  {
    "episode_id": 47118,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 72,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I can do the positive, the negative, and the politics. I mean, the positive is for a state like North Dakota, when people talk about going back to Trump era permitting, what they're talking about is NEPA, SHOCK, CLOCK, page limits, one agency review, reasonable amount of time, one year for an EA, two years for an EIS. All of that is now codified. That is now in law. for a state like North Dakota, that is going to be fantastic. We have learned from previous things like Obamacare, when you codify things, it's very difficult to roll them back. I think the politics of this is pretty interesting because mandatory spending was off the table. I mean, it was off the table on both sides. President Trump's running for president saying you can't cut Social Security, Medicare, all of those different issues. Both sides agreed that given the nature and the dangerous area of the world that military is pretty much off the table, and we plussed up veterans. So you're dealing with 11% of the budget, 11%. mandatory spending 70%, 75% of the budget. When you add military and veterans into it, you're down to 11% of the budget. And in 11% of the budget, we got $2.1 trillion in cuts in the next six years and got meaningful, substantive, responsible, Permitting reform work requirement reform and all of those things so I I mean I agree with a lot of my friends on the conservative the far conservative side of the house Except on one issue. I'm like we live in divided government. We have to get something that could pass",
