    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "No, it's not that volatile. It's emotional. I mean, obviously it was my bill on the floor two weeks ago that they held up trying to get across. So we deal with that. But I spent a lot of my time chat actually talking to other members of Congress that get really twirled up and emotional really quickly. You know, 10 years in criminal courtrooms across state. I just don't. I mean, whether that has the longest rule about history, was almost a day long and it was speaker Pelosi because the progressives revolted on. I believe it was the apartment foreclosures or the rent foreclosures during COVID, but I can't remember. You know, it never came up in the next election. The fact that the speaker Pelosi had the longest rule vote in the history of Congress. I think we have to see the force through the trees on this stuff. And it's the big things that matter. And we're always going to have fights and we're always going to have those types of issues. For me, like last week, it was interesting because I actually really, really hate the pistol brace rule. Regardless of how you feel about gun control or any of those issues, the ATF is essentially making 25 million Americans retroactive felons. So I didn't want that boat to get caught in the crossfire. with moderates versus conservatives. So that's what I said in Congress.",
