    "turn_idx": 95,
    "turn_text": "on the same way in the same way. And I mean, it's not. It's not comparable, but in the same way that Donald Trump, every time he got impeached or whatever, would similarly, and I guess that is one thing, and maybe this will be the last question for you, Kelly. I really worry about the amount of time Republicans are spending investigating Democrats' advice first, and I realize that some of that stuff is genuine and real and there are people that need to be held accountable and there are real problems but on the other hand like I worry that we are normalizing for instance I mean it's one thing to censor somebody it's another thing to find them I mean is that a new thing we're gonna start trying to do is levying these these devastating financial fines on people for whether whether they could be upheld in court or not. Are we going to investigate? Is every presidential campaign going to come with each candidate, you know, vowing to criminally investigate the other? I feel like we're crossing boundaries with a lot of this stuff. And I'd like to just pull back, you know, the question of pardon me, I know a lot of the candidates, including Doug Bergham, have been asked, you know, the question about whether or not they would pardon Donald Trump, and I actually like Doug Burgum's answer. I thought it was good for him to say, listen, that's a hypothetical. You know, the criminal justice process is not played out yet. And by the way, he can't be pardoned if he's not convicted. So, you know, I thought his people said he dodged the question. I actually didn't think that was appropriate. I didn't think he dodged it. I thought he gave a very nuanced answer, but I worry that we're getting to a point where it's just, does that worry you at all? And again, if somebody who worked in criminal justice on the defense side, does that worry you?",
