    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 100,
    "turn_text": "I don't think so. I will say this. I mean, you know, there's always talk about the convention and the relevance. And I think one of the things that we forget, and I know this because I was a state party chair, you have a district committee and a state committee that's made up of 47 district chairs and varying positions. And they all want elected officials to abide by honor, do everything they can for the convention. But at the same time, you have a vast number of those state committee members that don't follow those rules. It's nothing personal against Rebecca or any of those other people. If the ND GOP wants their state convention to be relevant, they should probably pass a law that requires the state committee at least to support their own endorsed candidates. I know this because I got into a fight and I made a district chair resign. When I was the state party chair, I made him resign because he wasn't endorsing his own endorsed candidates and actually had filed the run against one of them. And I think you can't do that and still be a part of the state committee. And it didn't make a lot of press, but it was a big fight internally. So, I mean, we'll figure it out. Like I said, I don't stress out a lot about any of this stuff. We're just going to keep moving forward and we'll go win the convention. And then if we have to, we'll go win a primary.",
