    "turn_idx": 107,
    "turn_text": "You know where I was, was ensconced at my desk indoors. uh... not enjoying the outside and instead writing about this stuff but i'm sorry it's i would have done it if i didn't think it was important and it's important because i mean on one hand you can look at the door to go to a public party and and say that i think people have a false notion about how important it is in politics. The party serves some important roles, but in terms of the candidates who are already in office, the party doesn't raise money for any of the candidates, really. The party does very little, in fact, to help them. There's some recruitment things that go on. Obviously, there's some coordination with the national party. There's media bias. There's organizing, putting out campaign signs and stuff like that. The impact is minimal. You know, symbolically, though rhetorically, being able to speak as the chairman of the North Dakota Republican Party, you know, that's going to carry some weight. And what's happened is Sandy Sanford, who is a outspoken culture warrior. I think that's the right thing. She was an activist involved with a group called North Dakota Can. that promoted, you know, the book bands, the pronoun bills, a lot of that stuff during the last legislative session. I know she was very active, particularly during the pandemic and being critical of Governor Doug Bergam. I should say that the lieutenant governor, former lieutenant governor, Prince Sanford, Sandy Sanford's husband, has denied to me that his wife's criticisms of his governor were a reason why he left the administration. I don't know. She was sharply critical with him, and he left before the term was up. So I don't know. I don't know what to make of all that. She has called for a primary challenger to our guest who just left, Kelly Armstrong. She endorsed Rick Becker over John Hovin, although John Hovin received the EDGOP's Convention Endorsement Last Cycle. She's I mean, that's that's where we're that's where we're at. So I mean, it's a very unique situation where you have a faction of the Republican Party that does not like a lot of the top elected Republicans and they're now in charge of the party. What happens? What happens now?",
