    "turn_idx": 116,
    "turn_text": "talked to a couple who are, their minds are heading in that direction. I won't say who because we were speaking off the record, but I can also tell you. So there's kind of two tiers in terms of the impact that this is going to have on elected Republicans. And you have sort of the statewide elected office holders. And I guess there's even two tiers there where you talk about, you know, I obviously like Governor Burgum and then the congressional delegation Kevin Kramer, John Hov and Kelly Armstrong, they're kind of in a tier in their own. And then you have, you know, the insurance commissioner and maybe the attorney general and the treasurer I have talked to some of the statewides and they have expressed concern about showing up at the convention. One of them told me, I don't want to be standing on stage while somebody says something crazy at the convention, right? Like I don't want to be like, like there's video of them saying this crazy thing and now I'm in the background on the video for all time. I don't want that. Several of them said that, you know, they're already going to start, they're going to start fundraising, fully expecting primary challenges from their own party. I mean, we're really kind of in a situation where, you know, some of the top elected Republicans in the state are preparing for their biggest electoral challenge, this cycle coming not from Democrats, but from their own party. That's a very interesting perspective. But then you go down the ballot and the legislature, the legislative candidates, whether they're incumbents or they're new people getting in, They're the ones who are probably going to be influenced by this the most, where you see that the party can probably have more influence over those candidates, just because often they don't raise a ton of money. Their races are not decided by a whole lot of votes. You know, that's one thing. And so one interesting thing that I wanted to get your thoughts on too was, was one of the things this, the, the meeting, the reorganization meeting where Sandy Sandberg was elected, that was on Friday. And by the way, it wasn't just Sandy Sandberg. John Trandam was elected vice chair. You go down the list. I mean, it was a clean sweep, the entire, the treasurer, the secretary, you know, it was a clean sweep. So it's, it's all the party, the entire party leadership is now in control of of whatever you want to call them the Bastia caucus, the culture warriors, the ultra mega people, that's where it's at. One of the things they're already kicking around is the creation of what's called a legislative review committee where they will essentially score a lawmaker. I don't know how it would function, but I guess they would identify lawmakers who are from their perspective, insufficiently, ideologically pure, And then I don't know, censoring them or saying that they can't receive party resources or something. And from their perspective, it's like, well, we just want to hold people accountable to the party platform. My argument is, well, now you're going to operate like the Communist Party does in China, where people who hold public office are supposed to be loyal to the party, not necessarily to the people who elected them. If it's the Republican Party platform, there are Republicans who get elected in, say, Fargo area districts that are very purple, that are not rock-red districts, who are going to have to listen to their constituents and maybe veer a little bit. There's not going to be a lot of tolerance to the North to a Republican Party for that. Anyway, I've said a lot of stuff, Chad, but",
