  {
    "episode_id": 47118,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 144,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. Well, and to me, it's offensive to me because I don't think that the American notion of public service works that way. I like political parties. I think they serve a very important function. But that function is more as sort of like a social club where the people who are republican candidates they're not loyal to the party they're loyal to their constituents with the party tries to do is identify candidates who are going to support a platform of ideas or certainly support more of those ideas maybe than another candidate would. Not to enforce absolute ideological loyalty to the party. Again, that's what communists do. That's not what how it's supposed to work in the American system of government. So I find it obnoxious, even whether it's Democrats that were doing it or Republicans that were doing it, it's obnoxious.",
