    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 166,
    "turn_text": "And that's one thing I'm interested is like, how aggressively are they going to pursue this? How aggressively are they going to go after it? I don't know. What I think is that if they get really aggressive, what they may end up proving is just how impotent they really are. in terms of being able to, because again, the state-wides can just skip the state convention and go straight to the June primary. They gather their signatures, they put their name on the ballot, that's it. And that's why you're seeing some, for instance, John Trandam, who's one of the new vice-chairs of the party, he wants to pass a law so that we identify on the ballot, who's the convention endorsed candidate versus who just gathered signatures to go on the June primary. I don't support that. I think that's a little silly. The other part of the story that came out this weekend, which I think is really interesting, had to do with North Dakota Right to Life, which is one of the... I don't know what you want to call them, but they're well known. They've been around for a long time. As you know, by their name, they advocate on the abortion issue. They had a very good legislative session. And North Dakota Right to Life participated in something that I thought was very good politics and was heartening.",
