  {
    "episode_id": 47118,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 168,
    "turn_text": "after the Dobbs decision came down, what North Dakota writes a life and other pro-life groups such as the Catholic Conference and some others. What they did is they said, let's identify some policies that we can all agree on, whether you're pro-choice or pro-life. It's gonna be some things that we all agree on. And they're gonna be focused on helping pregnant moms or new moms. And so it was things like expanding TANF for pregnant moms. It was exempting child diapers from the sales tax. Pretty low-hanging fruit, I think. Pretty good stuff. Now, the problem is you had some culture warrior lawmakers, such as Representative Jeff Hoversen, Representative Lori Van Winkle, Senator Jeff Magram, who voted against some of those bills. Well, in order to quote a right to life, scored those bills. And when I say scored those bills, a lot of these issue groups issue what's called a scorecard where they identify legislation that that represents their agenda, and then they calculate who voted for them, who voted against them, and then they produce a score, like how pro life was this person? Well, that because those lawmakers voted against those bills, they got a less than perfect rating from North Dakota right to life. And it made them mad. And so they complained to the board, and I have board president in my column today, I have board president, Lancer Labar, telling me that that was a factor that they ended up losing their entire staff over it. Um, they, they decided not to renew the contracts of like six staffers they had on contract during the session. And then their executive director and the director opted saying, you know, listen, if you're not going to support our staff, the, you know, the organization's doing great financially. We just held a very high profile fundraiser. We're going to go to, and then it turns out that two of the people in North Dakota, right to life board are new Andy GOP chairwoman, Sandy Sanford and Donna Magram, who's the wife of, of Senator Jeff Magram. And so I'm looking at that what happened in order to go to write to life. It's sort of a microcosm of maybe what's to come where there's going to be an active effort to sort of purge people who are seen as squishes or seen as inconvenient for the culture warrior faction.",
